# iter4 — business requirements

- **B1.** One person can stand up the whole data side with one command, on a laptop (Docker Desktop) or a small cloud VM, and point any engine at it.
- **B2.** Nothing already recorded is lost in the move: every iter3 work item, detail row, agent, tooling row, project, engine, user, lock, spend row and change counter arrives in iter4 unchanged.
- **B3.** The map of the program is built from the files already in the repository; nobody hand-enters it, and it keeps up with the files on its own.
- **B4.** A test group that goes red becomes exactly one work item, not one per sweep.
- **B5.** A node file keeps its identity through renames and moves.
- **B6.** One page, three views — Intro, Work queue, Project graph — under one header that always says which project is on screen; switching views keeps the project.
- **B7.** Anyone can see how the system fits together without reading code: contexts as areas, containers inside them, components inside those, the connections between them, and each use case as numbered steps.
- **B8.** A live project another team owns can be mapped without touching its files.
- **B9.** A new project can be grown from the map: new areas and parts (with their requirement and test files), connections, project-wide requirements and use cases, and one-off test runs — every change recorded as a work item, applied by an engine, committed, and visible on the map afterwards.
- **B10.** Testing runs through the same queue as the work: most test runs are deterministic (no AI), carried by the `test` agent; red results become work items.
- **B11.** A newcomer can learn what iter is and why it matters (business view), how it works (technical view), and stand up a project with a guided wizard, from inside the app.

